static const QaspHelp::Lesson g_Lessons[] =
{
    { "forces", "Start here", "The four forces", "What keeps an aircraft moving and supported?", QaspHelp::Diagram::Forces, "Lift and weight act roughly up and down in straight, level flight. Thrust pushes forward; drag resists the motion through air.", "To keep a steady speed and height in this simple case, lift balances weight and thrust balances drag. An imbalance changes motion.", "If forward thrust is 1,200 N and drag is 1,000 N, 200 N remains to accelerate the aircraft.", "Hover over Thrust in the VSPAERO Disk controls. Read its meaning, effect and example.", "The picture describes straight, level flight. Forces tilt during turns and climbs; this is not a complete flight simulator.", "lift weight thrust drag balance force newton" },
    { "thrust", "Aircraft basics", "Thrust and power", "Push is not the same thing as energy per second.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Thrust, "Thrust is the pushing or pulling force from a propeller or jet. Power is how quickly an engine or motor supplies energy.", "Extra forward thrust can help you accelerate or climb. How much thrust a propeller makes depends on its blades, RPM, air density and flight speed.", "An engine with more power does not automatically produce proportionally more thrust. The propeller must use that power at the required speed.", "Open Analysis > Aero > VSPAERO, then Disk. Hover over Thrust, Power, RPM and Efficiency before changing an input.", "Entering a thrust value describes an assumed operating point. It does not prove a real engine can deliver it.", "engine propulsion motor horsepower watts propeller rotor" },
    { "lift", "Aircraft basics", "Lift, angle and stall", "Why raising the nose does not always make more lift.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Angle, "Angle of attack is the angle between a wing reference line and the air arriving at it. Lift is the aerodynamic force perpendicular to that airflow.", "Increasing angle usually increases lift at first. Beyond the real wing's stall angle, separated airflow can reduce lift and control authority.", "An aircraft can have a high angle of attack with its nose almost level if its flight path is descending.", "In VSPAERO, start with a small Alpha range. Compare CL and drag, and check the selected stall treatment.", "Do not treat a linear lift curve at large angles as proof that the wing cannot stall.", "alpha CL maximum lift CLmax stall airfoil pitch" },
    { "drag", "Aircraft basics", "Drag", "The air resistance your engine must overcome.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Drag, "Drag acts opposite the motion through air. Skin friction and shape cause parasite drag; producing lift also causes induced drag.", "At fixed shape and coefficients, doubling true airspeed makes aerodynamic forces four times larger. The coefficients themselves may also change.", "A larger wing may help slow flight but increase skin area. A longer span can reduce lift-related drag at the same lift and speed.", "Use Parasite Drag for a smooth-body estimate. Use VSPAERO for lift-related effects; understand the method before combining results.", "Do not add results blindly: overlap between drag methods can double-count some contributions.", "CD CD0 induced parasite friction resistance" },
    { "span-area", "Aircraft basics", "Span, area and aspect ratio", "Three different ways to describe a wing.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Wing, "Span is tip-to-tip wing width. For a flat wing, area is the outline's size seen from above. Aspect ratio describes how long and narrow it is: span squared divided by area.", "More area can lower stall speed for the same weight and maximum lift coefficient. More span generally reduces lift-related drag, but adds structural demands.", "A rectangular wing with 10 m span and 1.5 m chord has 15 square metres of area and an aspect ratio of about 6.7.", "Select a Wing and compare Plan totals with Sect values. A section is not necessarily the complete mirrored wing.", "These are geometry values, not guarantees of flight performance. Check units, symmetry and mass.", "TotalSpan TotalArea TotalAR wing loading mirrored root tip" },
    { "chord-taper", "Aircraft basics", "Chord and taper", "Front to back is not the same as thickness.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Taper, "Chord is the straight-line distance from the wing's front edge to its back edge. Taper is tip chord divided by root chord.", "Changing chord changes wing area. Reducing the tip chord makes a narrower tip; stall behaviour still depends on airfoil shape and twist.", "A 2 m root chord and a 1 m tip chord give a taper ratio of 0.5. A taper of 1 means equal chords.", "On the Wing Sect tab, hover over Root Chord, Tip Chord and Taper. Change one allowed input and watch the calculated values.", "Only certain combinations are independent. A greyed-out geometry value may be calculated from the selected drivers.", "root tip Avg_Chord driver span" },
    { "sweep", "Aircraft basics", "Sweep", "Why some wings lean backward.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Sweep, "Sweep is the backward or forward angle of a wing measurement line when viewed from above.", "Sweep can help at high speed by changing compressibility effects, but it has low-speed and structural trade-offs. It is not a universal speed upgrade.", "Leading-edge sweep and quarter-chord sweep are different measurements on a tapered wing.", "In Wing > Sect, check Sweep Location before comparing angles. 0 is the leading edge; 0.25 is quarter-chord.", "An unswept quarter-chord line can still have swept-looking leading and trailing edges.", "Sweep_Location Sec_Sweep leading edge quarter chord" },
    { "dihedral-twist", "Aircraft basics", "Dihedral and twist", "Two different kinds of wing angle.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Dihedral, "Dihedral is the wing's upward tilt seen from the front. Twist changes the airfoil angle as you move along the wing.", "Dihedral affects response to sideslip. Tip washout means a lower tip angle and can help the root stall before the tips, depending on the whole design.", "A shallow V seen head-on is dihedral. A tip angled 2 degrees below the root has 2 degrees of washout.", "Change these separately on the Wing Sect tab and inspect the model from the front and side.", "Positive dihedral is not a promise that the aircraft will level itself. Stability depends on the complete aircraft.", "roll sideslip stability washout Twist relative absolute" },
    { "airfoil", "Aircraft basics", "Airfoil, camber and thickness", "The shape of one slice through a wing.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Airfoil, "An airfoil is the wing cross-section. Camber is the bend of its middle line. Thickness ratio is maximum thickness divided by chord.", "These change lift, drag, pitching behaviour and room for structure. Chord and thickness are separate dimensions.", "With a 1 m chord and thickness ratio 0.12, maximum thickness is about 0.12 m. Camber 0.02 means 2 percent of chord.", "Open a Wing's Airfoil tab. Compare the shape before and after a small thickness or camber change.", "The drawn shape alone does not tell you its maximum lift or stall characteristics. Use appropriate aerodynamic data.", "NACA ThickChord CamberLoc Chord CLmax" },
    { "cg", "Aircraft basics", "Mass and centre of gravity", "Where the aircraft balances matters as much as its size.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Cg, "Mass is how much matter the aircraft contains. Weight is the gravity force acting on it. Centre of gravity, or CG, is its mass balance point.", "Moving a heavy part shifts the CG. Whether the aircraft can be trimmed and remain stable depends on aerodynamic forces and moments too.", "A 10 kg battery moved rearward shifts the CG rearward; the amount depends on the other masses and their positions.", "Set meaningful mass assumptions on component Mass tabs, then run Analysis > Mass Prop and enable Draw Cg.", "A visible CG marker is not a safe CG envelope. QASP has not certified a stable or controllable aircraft.", "weight density battery balance trim stability inertia" },
    { "units", "Using QASP", "Units and model size", "A number such as 10 does not automatically mean metres.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Units, "Geometry uses numerical length units. Analysis and export tools need a consistent interpretation such as metres or feet.", "A unit mismatch can make the same model look plausible while giving incorrect size, Reynolds number, mass or forces.", "If a fuselage length of 7 means 7 metres, use metres consistently where the analysis asks for geometry units.", "Write your chosen length and mass units in your project notes. Check each analysis's unit selections before running it.", "Do not assume changing a unit selector rescales existing geometry. Check what that particular control does.", "metres meters feet SI imperial conversion scale" },
    { "position", "Using QASP", "Position and rotation", "Move a part without accidentally resizing it.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Axes, "X, Y and Z are the model's coordinate directions. Position moves a component; rotation turns it. Relative coordinates refer to its parent.", "Moving a wing or tail changes aircraft layout and can change balance and aerodynamic moments. It does not automatically resize the part.", "To make a single vertical fin, rotate a Wing about X on XForm and disable the mirror that would overlap it.", "Use the XForm inputs for accurate placement. View > Fit All On Screen brings the model back into view.", "Do not rely on dragging in the 3D view to move geometry; view rotation and component placement are different actions.", "XForm X_Rotation Y Z translate attach parent" },
    { "first-body", "Build a first model", "1. Start with the body", "A shape exercise, not a flight-ready aircraft.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Body, "A fuselage is the main body. QASP builds it from a series of cross-sections, like slices through a loaf.", "The section sizes and positions determine space for payload and the exterior shape. A longer body does not automatically assign more real mass.", "For practice, choose metres and sketch a body about 7 m long. This example size is not a recommendation for a real aircraft.", "File > New. Open Model > Geometry, choose Fuselage, and press Add. In its Design tab, set Length; inspect section widths and heights.", "Save often as .vsp3. Undo Parameter Change reverses supported parameter edits, not every modelling action.", "first aircraft tutorial fuselage beginner new add" },
    { "first-wing", "Build a first model", "2. Add and size a wing", "Choose independent inputs, then inspect the totals.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Wing, "A Wing component can represent a main wing, tail or fin. Its Sect tab describes individual sections; Plan shows totals.", "The selected drivers are the values you control. The remaining shape values are calculated so the section stays consistent.", "For a practice rectangular wing, use section span 5, root chord 1.5 and tip chord 1.5. XZ symmetry gives about 10 total span and 15 area.", "Choose Wing in Model > Geometry and press Add. On Sect, select Span, Root Chord and Tip Chord as drivers. Set sweep to 0 and enter the example values.", "Check Plan totals and symmetry rather than assuming section values describe the whole aircraft. This is only a geometry example.", "tutorial drivers rectangular Area Aspect 10 15" },
    { "first-tail", "Build a first model", "3. Add tails and save", "Use the same Wing tool for different lifting surfaces.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Tail, "A horizontal tail helps control nose-up and nose-down motion. A vertical fin helps control nose-left and nose-right motion.", "Tail size and distance from the CG affect available control moments. Tail volume, trim and stability need proper analysis.", "Add a smaller Wing behind the main wing as a horizontal tail. Add a separate Wing rotated about X for a fin.", "Position parts with XForm. Turn off XZ symmetry for a single fin on the centreline. Use File > Save As and keep the .vsp3 model.", "Adding tails does not prove the aircraft is stable. This lesson deliberately gives no supposedly safe tail size.", "tutorial horizontal vertical fin rudder elevator tailplane save" },
    { "compgeom", "Analysis tools", "CompGeom: shape checks", "A geometry check, not a flight test.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Measure, "CompGeom intersects component surfaces and reports geometric quantities such as areas and volumes.", "It can help identify problems with the model and supplies useful geometric information. It does not calculate a maximum speed or altitude.", "If two components overlap unexpectedly, their exposed areas and volumes may not match a simple sum of the separate shapes.", "Open Analysis > CompGeom. Choose the intended geometry set, compute, and inspect the messages and output.", "It is not a mandatory first step for every other analysis. Each tool has its own geometry preparation and assumptions.", "mesh intersection wetted area volume compute" },
    { "massprop", "Analysis tools", "Mass Prop: calculate balance", "Give the tool mass assumptions first.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Cg, "Mass Properties estimates total mass, CG and resistance to rotation, called inertia, from the included geometry and mass inputs.", "The result depends on your material density, shell mass, point masses and selected sets. It cannot discover the weight of an unmodelled battery.", "Model a skin by mass per area, or a solid by mass per volume. Check that you have not counted both unintentionally.", "Enter component mass assumptions. Open Analysis > Mass Prop, select the intended sets, then Compute and Draw Cg.", "A calculated mass is only as good as the inputs. It is not a weighed aircraft or a validated stability result.", "density shell inertia Ixx Iyy Izz balance CG" },
    { "parasite", "Analysis tools", "Parasite Drag: estimate drag", "Estimate one important part of the drag budget.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Drag, "Parasite Drag estimates skin-friction and shape-related drag using geometry, atmosphere inputs and empirical equations.", "The result changes with speed, altitude, surface condition and reference area. It does not include every source of real-aircraft drag.", "Run the same shape at two speeds or altitudes while keeping unit choices and reference area consistent.", "Open Analysis > Aero > Parasite Drag. Check geometry length units, airspeed units, atmosphere, reference area and included components.", "This result alone cannot give top speed, climb rate or maximum altitude. Lift-related drag and available propulsion are also needed.", "CD0 drag counts laminar turbulent friction atmosphere" },
    { "vspaero", "Analysis tools", "VSPAERO: airflow and loads", "Ask a specific question at a specific flight condition.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Angle, "VSPAERO estimates aerodynamic forces and moments using a prepared model and chosen flight conditions.", "You can study lift, drag, pressure and stability-related results within the method's assumptions. The solver does not automatically validate a complete aircraft.", "An Alpha range of 0 to 8 with 5 cases requests 0, 2, 4, 6 and 8 degrees. A real stall prediction needs suitable modelling and validation.", "Open Analysis > Aero > VSPAERO. Check included geometry, reference quantities, flow conditions and solver messages before trusting a plot.", "Wait for results to converge and check resolution. A completed run can still contain physically unsuitable assumptions.", "alpha Mach Reynolds CL CD moments convergence solver" },
    { "results", "Analysis tools", "Read CL, CD and L/D", "Comparison numbers are not forces by themselves.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Results, "CL is a lift coefficient. CD is a drag coefficient. They express forces relative to air density, speed and reference area. L/D compares lift with drag.", "A larger L/D means less drag for the same lift at that condition, but says nothing by itself about takeoff safety or engine capability.", "If CL is 0.6 and CD is 0.03 using the same references, L/D is 20. To obtain a force, density, speed and reference area are still needed.", "Check the flight condition and reference area before comparing plots or coefficients from different tools.", "Do not compare L/D across different configurations without understanding what drag contributions each result includes.", "lift drag polar coefficient efficiency reference area" },
    { "altitude", "Analysis tools", "How high or fast can it fly?", "Know when the available information is not enough.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Altitude, "Maximum speed depends on where available thrust balances total drag. Climbing needs excess propulsive capability as well as adequate lift.", "A useful altitude estimate needs aircraft weight, a validated drag and lift model, and engine or motor performance as altitude and speed change.", "Typing 5,000 m into an atmosphere input only requests conditions at 5,000 m. It is not a calculated ceiling.", "Collect consistent mass, aerodynamic and propulsion data before making a performance estimate. The current QASP build has no validated automatic ceiling calculation.", "Do not interpret an atmosphere setting or a successful solver run as a claim that a real aircraft can reach that altitude.", "ceiling max maximum altitude top speed climb performance thrust power" },
    { "mesh", "Analysis tools", "Meshes and external solvers", "A mesh prepares a calculation; it is not the answer.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Mesh, "A mesh divides surfaces into small pieces. CFD Mesh prepares aerodynamic geometry; FEA Structure prepares structural modelling data.", "Resolution, intersections, materials, loads and boundary conditions all affect the later solver results.", "More triangles may improve geometric detail, but they cannot fix the wrong load case or missing material data.", "Use the relevant mesh tool for a defined analysis task and inspect its warnings and exported files.", "Generating a mesh does not calculate structural strength or certify an aircraft. External solving and validation may still be required.", "CFD FEA structure triangles grid external solver stress" },
    { "other-tools", "Analysis tools", "Which other analysis tool?", "Pick a tool by the question it answers.", QaspHelp::Diagram::Workflow, "Planar Slice measures cross-sections. Projected Area measures the silhouette from a chosen direction. Geometry Analysis checks geometric relationships.", "DegenGeom creates simplified geometry data. Trimmed Surfaces prepares intersected surfaces. Wave Drag addresses a particular high-speed drag method.", "Use Projected Area for a silhouette-area question, not as a substitute for aerodynamic lift or total drag.", "Open the corresponding Analysis menu item and check its inputs and reference documentation before computing.", "No one tool proves that the aircraft flies. Do not assume every tool must be run in a fixed sequence.", "planar slice projected area degengeom trimmed surfaces wave drag analysis order" },
